**Gout** is a type of arthritis. It happens when too much **uric acid (urate)** builds up in the blood and forms sharp **crystals** in and around the joints. It affects around **1 in 40 adults** in the UK and is more common in men.

## Symptoms

A gout attack (also called a **flare**) usually comes on quickly, often at night. The joint becomes:

- **very painful**, so that even a bed sheet touching it hurts
- **hot, swollen and red**

It most often affects the **big toe**, but can affect the feet, ankles, knees, hands, wrists and elbows. Over time, lumps of crystals called **tophi** can form under the skin.

## What raises the risk?

- family history
- being overweight
- **kidney disease**, high blood pressure, diabetes or heart disease
- some medicines, such as **water tablets (diuretics)**
- drinking **alcohol**, especially beer and spirits
- sugary drinks and a diet high in red meat, offal and some seafood

## Getting diagnosed

A GP will usually diagnose gout from your symptoms and a **blood test** to measure urate, which is best done a few weeks after a flare. Sometimes fluid is taken from the joint to look for crystals.

Read about [gout treatment](/conditions/musculoskeletal/gout/treatment/).

{% callout type="warn" title="See a doctor urgently" %}
Call **NHS 111** or see a GP the same day if a swollen, painful joint comes with a **high temperature** or you feel unwell. This can be a sign of a joint infection, which needs urgent treatment.
{% /callout %}
